<!-- Analytics — THE funnel: ten events, one policy, emitted through the framework's own
     plane and through nothing this file invented.

     WHAT THIS ANSWERS. This template's whole thesis is a monetisation funnel — hero →
     detail → free episodes → paywall → coins or VIP — and until this file the app emitted
     nothing at all, so not one step of it could be observed. You cannot tune a paywall you
     cannot measure, and a reference template that ships the paywall without the measurement
     teaches half a lesson.

     ══ THE TRANSPORT IS `Core/PostHog`, AND NOTHING ELSE IS INVENTED ══════════════════
     `dsx.module.posthog.capture({ event, properties })` is the framework's product-analytics
     verb — the one module in the tree with a (name, properties) shape and a real, SDK-free
     web implementation (its facet POSTs `/i/v0/e/` itself rather than injecting a vendor
     script). Two neighbours were considered and are NOT this:

       · `Core/Telemetry` is crash and error plumbing. Its `capture` drains the kernel error
         ledger and has no `event` argument anywhere in it. Wiring a funnel through it would
         file every purchase as an incident.
       · `Core/Firebase/Modules/Analytics` has the right verb and NO WEB LANE by design; its
         own manifest says so and points here: "Product analytics on the web is Core/PostHog."

     ══ CAPABILITY FIRST, PLATFORM SECOND (the AdGate rule) ════════════════════════════
     `has('posthog')` decides, never `os == 'web'`. With the module present the event is
     captured; without it the event drains to `dsx.log` — the framework diagnostic spine,
     console-shaped, credential keys masked, identical on all three renderers and mirrored
     into the kernel log ring the dev-center console streams. A DRAIN, not a transport:
     nothing leaves the device, and the Manage screen says exactly that.

     ══ ARTICLE 7, NAMED: NO BUILD CAN REACH THE SINK TODAY ════════════════════════════
     `Core/PostHog` ships `web/index.js` complete and its manifest declares no `web.entry`,
     so `cli/src/build.ts` (`if (pkg.entry === undefined) continue`) emits no chunk and
     `has('posthog')` is false on every web build. Same for `Core/Consent`. Not a new class
     of defect: `Core/SocialShare/dsx.json` records exactly it about itself — "every web
     build silently had has('share') === false and no chunk on disk, while the implementation
     sat there complete. Declaring the entry is the whole fix." SocialShare was fixed; these
     two were not. Filed as PLAN.md §6.92 — three lines per manifest.

     So `posthog` and `consent` are declared in dsx.config.json `modules`, the DECLARE-THEN-
     REACH list for platform schemes no configured package claims. That is what admits these
     calls to the linter while the native side provides the module. The day the entries land,
     the funnel starts sending and not one line here changes.

     ══ WHY EVERY EMITTER CARRIES ITS OWN FOUR-LINE ACTION ═════════════════════════════
     This was one global `track()` function first, and it did nothing at all, silently.
     Measured on a live origin, from inside a `<functions global="true">` body:

       dsx.log(msg)                         no console line, no ring entry
       dsx.module.route.push({path:'/x'})   no navigation, URL unchanged
       global.sdEvents = next               read back NULL, before and after

     while the identical three statements in an action body three lines away all worked.
     A functions body is the EXPRESSION tier: it computes and it reads, and every runner-only
     callee plus the store-write path is a no-op there. Filed as PLAN.md §6.94.

     So the SIDE EFFECT has to live in an action, and an action is component-local — six
     components emit, so six copies of the same four lines. What is NOT duplicated is the
     POLICY: `analyticsBlocked()` and `analyticsSink()` below are pure expressions, they work
     exactly where a functions body works, and they are the only place the consent rule and
     the sink choice are written down. Copy the shell; never re-decide the policy.

     ══ CONSENT IS A GATE, NOT A COMMENT ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
     `Core/Consent` publishes `granted` / `stored`. On the web lane a facet's declared
     variables land at `global.SCHEME.NAME` (kernel bus.ts: `state.set` writes
     `global.consent.granted`), and `dsx.module` context reads have no path in the web JSE at
     all, so the gate reads `global.consent.granted` and NOT the canonical native spelling.
     With the module absent there is no consent plane and the gate is open — the framework's
     own documented fail-open — which is correct for a build that also has no sink.

     ══ WHAT IS IN AN EVENT, AND WHAT IS NEVER ═════════════════════════════════════════
     Ids and numbers. A show id, an episode id, an episode index, a price in coins, a sku, an
     amount, a lane name, a boolean. NEVER: the viewer subject, an email, a display name, a
     token, a comment body, a search term, an IP, a device id. The funnel answers "how many
     people fell out between the paywall and the unlock", which needs no idea who they were,
     and `identify()` is deliberately never called anywhere in this app. Titles are absent
     too: a title is one join away from an id, and the id is the stable half.

     ══ THE TEN, AND WHY THESE TEN ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       app_open              once per session, from this mount
       show_detail_viewed    the detail payload landed (a mount is not a view)
       episode_started       the viewer reached an episode and got the clip
       paywall_shown         the viewer reached an episode and got the wall
       episode_completed     playback ran out, before any auto-advance decides
       unlock_attempted      a spend was asked for, episode or series
       unlock_succeeded      the server granted it, with what it cost
       purchase_started      a checkout was opened
       purchase_completed    the SETTLE, not the tap: money Stripe confirmed and the
                             server granted, counted once even when the recovery retries
       restore               both phases, so a restore that finds nothing is still visible

     `episode_started` and `paywall_shown` are the SAME boundary seen twice, which is the
     point: their ratio is the paywall conversion, and no other pair in this app measures it.

     ══ HOW A SURFACE EMITS ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     Copy the four-line shell into the emitting component, then

       dsx.action.track({ name: 'paywall_shown', props: { show: id, episode: ep.id } })

     Mount `<Analytics/>` beside `<Theme/>` at the top of a screen: the mount is what fires
     `app_open`, and it fires ONCE per session however many screens mount it. -->
<stack width="0" height="0" on:appear="dsx.action.opened()">
  <head>
    <!-- ONCE PER SESSION, not once per screen. Every screen mounts this part so a viewer
         landing on any deep link is counted, and the flag is what stops the twelfth mount
         filing a twelfth app_open. It rides `global` rather than a variable because a route
         change destroys the mount and its store with it. -->
    <action as="opened">
      if (global.sdOpened == true) { return }
      global.sdOpened = true
      dsx.action.track({ name: 'app_open', props: { lane: os } })
    </action>

    <!-- THE EMIT SHELL, and the reference copy of it. Four lines, repeated in every
         component that emits, because a functions body cannot carry a side effect (§6.94). -->
    <action as="track" inputs="name, props">
      if (analyticsBlocked()) { return }
      if (analyticsSink() != '') { dsx.module.posthog.capture({ event: name, properties: props }) }
      else { dsx.log('funnel', name, props) }
    </action>

    <!-- BODY CAVEAT (js-core.md): the native parsers do not lift a `<functions>` body as raw
         JS yet, so no bare `<` and no `&&` in here. Every guard is a separate `if`.
         PURE EXPRESSIONS ONLY, and that is a law rather than a style — see §6.94. -->
    <functions global="true">
      /* THE CONSENT GATE, written once. True when the viewer has a consent plane and has
         not granted. Absent module = no plane = open, which is the framework fail-open and
         is also correct in a build that has no sink to send to. */
      function analyticsBlocked() {
        if (has('consent') == false) { return false }
        if (global.consent == null) { return true }
        return global.consent.granted != true
      }

      /* WHICH SINK, IF ANY. Empty means the events are draining to dsx.log; the Manage
         screen reads this so it can name the degradation instead of implying a funnel is
         being recorded that is not. */
      function analyticsSink() {
        if (has('posthog')) { return 'posthog' }
        return ''
      }
    </functions>
  </head>
</stack>
